1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,242
Meet Bufo. Describe a scene, and this agent plans a drawing, renders

2
00:00:05,242 --> 00:00:09,983
it, and returns an image you can use as an emoji. You

3
00:00:09,983 --> 00:00:14,725
can change models or continue the conversation to refine the result. The

4
00:00:14,725 --> 00:00:19,467
challenge is consistency: Bufo should stay recognizable, and every requested detail should

5
00:00:19,467 --> 00:00:24,208
appear. A convincing response does not tell us whether the image actually

6
00:00:24,208 --> 00:00:25,394
meets those requirements.

7
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:30,959
Here is a concrete failure from our validation set. We asked for

8
00:00:30,959 --> 00:00:35,418
an airport marshaller holding two orange batons, an airplane behind Bufo, and

9
00:00:35,418 --> 00:00:39,877
a smaller observer on the right. The baseline rendered an image, but

10
00:00:39,877 --> 00:00:44,336
the airplane is not clearly established. To improve this app, we need

11
00:00:44,336 --> 00:00:48,795
to connect that failure to its execution, turn useful examples into training

12
00:00:48,795 --> 00:00:53,254
data, and measure whether a new model fixes the behavior. That is

13
00:00:53,254 --> 00:00:54,368
the Forge loop.

14
00:00:55,500 --> 00:01:00,493
Agent Lens gives us the complete conversation and trace. We can follow

15
00:01:00,493 --> 00:01:05,487
the request through planning and rendering to the final PNG, and inspect

16
00:01:05,487 --> 00:01:10,480
which model produced it. This separates execution problems from visual quality problems.

17
00:01:10,480 --> 00:01:15,474
Our saved image evaluations check character similarity and requested details; a successful

18
00:01:15,474 --> 00:01:20,467
render alone is not enough. The trace gives us the context we

19
00:01:20,467 --> 00:01:22,964
need to investigate and curate examples.

20
00:01:24,083 --> 00:01:29,420
For this iteration, we collected and reviewed teacher examples from GPT six

21
00:01:29,420 --> 00:01:34,758
point one Sol. This dataset contains nine hundred and eleven trajectories, including

22
00:01:34,758 --> 00:01:40,095
the request, planning, drawing tool arguments, tool results, and final response. Keeping

23
00:01:40,095 --> 00:01:45,432
the trajectory matters: the student must learn how to construct the scene,

24
00:01:45,432 --> 00:01:50,769
not just write a caption. These reviews include automated and assistant review.

25
00:01:50,769 --> 00:01:55,661
The separate blinded human confirmation queue has not yet been completed.

26
00:01:56,792 --> 00:02:01,466
Forge Post Training connects that dataset to managed distillation. Our student is

27
00:02:01,466 --> 00:02:06,140
Qwen three point eight, twenty seven B. The selected run trains all

28
00:02:06,140 --> 00:02:10,814
assistant turns for one epoch, with batch size two and learning rate

29
00:02:10,814 --> 00:02:15,488
zero point zero zero zero one. The dataset, training run, and versioned

30
00:02:15,488 --> 00:02:20,162
checkpoint stay linked. We also tested three epochs and a reinforcement learning

31
00:02:20,162 --> 00:02:24,836
update. Neither beat the selected checkpoint on aggregate validation passes. Each experiment

32
00:02:24,836 --> 00:02:27,952
has to earn its place in the app.

33
00:02:29,083 --> 00:02:34,594
On forty eight validation prompts, base Qwen passed seventeen. The one epoch

34
00:02:34,594 --> 00:02:40,104
student passed thirty: an increase from thirty five point four to sixty

35
00:02:40,104 --> 00:02:45,614
two point five percent. Three epochs passed twenty seven; reinforcement learning matched

36
00:02:45,614 --> 00:02:51,125
thirty. We compare requirement coverage, character similarity, render validity, and latency. This

37
00:02:51,125 --> 00:02:56,635
is a measured improvement, with failures still remaining. It does not establish

38
00:02:56,635 --> 00:03:02,145
Sol parity: that historical reference used different runtime settings. Human confirmation remains

39
00:03:02,145 --> 00:03:03,523
the next check.

40
00:03:04,625 --> 00:03:09,286
Return to the airport example. The fine tuned checkpoint now produces a

41
00:03:09,286 --> 00:03:13,946
recognizable airplane behind Bufo, along with the batons and smaller observer. Its

42
00:03:13,946 --> 00:03:18,607
saved verdict passes. This example makes the improvement visible, while the full

43
00:03:18,607 --> 00:03:23,268
evaluation tells us how broadly it holds. Both the drawing and the

44
00:03:23,268 --> 00:03:27,929
trace remain available, so we can challenge the score instead of taking

45
00:03:27,929 --> 00:03:29,094
it on faith.

46
00:03:30,208 --> 00:03:35,131
Back in Bufo, the fine tuned model is available alongside the baseline,

47
00:03:35,131 --> 00:03:40,054
using its exact model name. We can try scenes and keep refining

48
00:03:40,054 --> 00:03:44,977
the conversation. New failures give us the next examples to investigate. That

49
00:03:44,977 --> 00:03:49,900
is how this project uses Forge: run the app, observe its behavior,

50
00:03:49,900 --> 00:03:54,822
curate useful trajectories, improve the model, and evaluate the result before advancing

51
00:03:54,822 --> 00:03:59,745
it. The loop stays grounded in the image the user actually receives.
